(* README *)

1 - Summary
    -------

    A002 formalizes CARRYLESS SEQUENT: a certified checker for closed
    implicational Hilbert derivations with axiom schemas K and S and modus
    ponens. Two theorems connect its executable checker to an inductive proof
    judgment:

      - `normalized_verifyb_iff`       : Boolean checking reflects validity
      - `certified_verify_accept_sound`: arithmetic acceptance implies validity


2 - State
    -----

                      ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
                      │                x                 │
                      │   └─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┘ 7 / 10   │
                      └──────────────────────────────────┘

    Evaluation: 7, Serviceable. Effective, mechanized, documented. Proof
    tactics and prose are not optimal. Not peer reviewed.


3 - Intuition
    ---------

    A normalized derivation is a list of formulas annotated with one of three
    rules:

      K:   A -> (B -> A)

      S:   (A -> (B -> C)) -> ((A -> B) -> (A -> C))

      MP:  prefix[p] = A     prefix[q] = A -> B
           -------------------------------------
                             B

    The checker reads the list from left to right. MP citations are lookups in
    the accepted prefix, so no line can cite itself or a future line. The final
    formula must equal the requested target.

      lines, target
           │
           ▼
      normalized_verifyb ── true ──▶ NormalizedProof lines target

    This correspondence is exact:

      normalized_verifyb lines target = true
        <->
      NormalizedProof lines target.

    The public entry point accepts natural-number codes:

      A002_Verify_certified : nat -> nat -> nat.

    It first converts the coded formula and derivation into inductive normal
    forms, then runs the proved checker. A001 decoding is total, so a decoded
    pair is used structurally only when re-encoding returns the original code:

      canonical001b c = Nat.eqb (encode (fst001 c) (snd001 c)) c.

    This guard prevents malformed arithmetic inputs from being mistaken for
    syntax. Conversely, every `NormalizedProof` has a canonical arithmetic
    quotation accepted by `A002_Verify_certified`.


